## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of three-valued logic, constraint propagation, and backtracking in a realistic feature-flag style puzzle. Requires translating rules into propagation steps, handling indeterminate values, and proving existence or absence of a solution.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued logic
- Constraint propagation with forward checking
- Backtracking search and domain pruning

## Code Example
```javascript
function not(v){ return v==='T'?'F':v==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function nub(a,b){ // a,b in 'T','F','U'; top value is 'T'
  // define max (∨) and min (∧)
  const order = {'F':0,'U':1,'T':2};
  const max = (x,y) => order[x] >= order[y] ? x : y;
  const min = (x,y) => order[x] <= order[y] ? x : y;
  return {or: max(a,b), and: min(a,b)};
}
```

## Why This Question Is Asked
Assesses the ability to design a constraint solver for 3-valued Kleene logic, demonstrating handling of partial knowledge and undefined states in logical policies.

## Key Concepts
- 3-valued truth tables for ¬, ∨, ∧, and →
- Arc-consistency enforcement on ternary domains
- Fixed-point propagation without backtracking

## Implementation
```javascript
const order = { 'F': 0, 'U': 1, 'T': 2 };

function not(v) {
  return v === 'F' ? 'T' : v === 'T' ? 'F' : 'U';
}

function max(a, b) {
  return order[a] > order[b] ? a : b;
}

function implies(a, b) {
  return max(not(a), b);
}

function supports(constraint, varIndex, value, currentAssignment) {
  const vars = ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D'];
  for (const val of ['F', 'U', 'T']) {
    currentAssignment[vars[varIndex]] = val;
    if (constraint(currentAssignment) !== 'F') return true;
  }
  return false;
}

function arcConsistency(domains, constraints) {
  let changed = true;
  while (changed) {
    changed = false;
    for (const constraint of constraints) {
      for (const [varName, domain] of Object.entries(domains)) {
        const filtered = domain.filter(val => 
          supports(constraint, varName, val, { A: domains.A[0], B: domains.B[0], C: domains.C[0], D: domains.D[0] })
        );
        if (filtered.length !== domain.length) {
          domains[varName] = filtered;
          changed = true;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return domains;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a tiny 3-valued solver: propagation, clash detection, and controlled backtracking while preserving non-boolean placeholders.

## Key Concepts
- 3-valued logic propagation
- Forward-checking and fixpoint computation
- Backtracking with Unknowns to preserve flexibility

## Code Example
```javascript
function propagate(vars, constraints) {
  // vars: {S1: T|F|U, S2: T|F|U, S3: T|F|U}
  // constraints: list of functions returning {changed, ok}
  // Minimal sketch for illustration
  let changed = true;
  while (changed) {
    changed = false;
    for (const c of constraints) {
      const res = c(vars);
      if (res.changed) changed = true;
      if (!res.ok) return {ok: false};
    }
  }
  return {ok: true, vars};
}
```

